WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.020
Loops!

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:03.420
It will tie you up in knots!

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:27.080
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:28.960
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:30.180
And I'm Joe.

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:34.120
Loops is another one of those puzzle-based NES video games.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.860
And I say another one of those, but we don't get these too often, but we just kinda got one with Pipe Dream.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:41.300
And this isn't like that far off.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:48.220
It's just weird. They're made by two separate companies, so it's just like bad timing, I guess, for one or the other, right?

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:54.960
This is definitely pipe adjacent, if not straight up pipe.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:58.140
Yeah, they look kinda like we're laying pipe.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.220
I was worried that we were gonna get there again.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.780
I was worried that we weren't.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:07.920
So we got it out of the way in the first minute.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.600
Loops has nothing to do with pipes.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:24.340
It's actually just creating, hence the title, loops, as you fit puzzle pieces to make closed loops on a kind of like confined claustrophobic screen.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:26.820
They don't really give you a full space.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:33.780
And it's not like, you know how Tetris is like a rectangle, but it's like a height-heavy rectangle?

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:34.920
This is a like...

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.500
Height-heavy rectangle.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.000
You know what I mean, right?

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:46.060
Like, the pieces are falling down on a rectangle that is standing upright like a monolith, whereas this rectangle...

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:48.700
We're looking down on a board now.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:52.620
Yes, this rectangle is like you're looking into a room or something from the top down.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:57.600
So it's not the way I'm used to looking at rectangles on my NES.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:03.700
And so everything just feels a bit closed in and narrow here as you...

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:07.120
You know, the goal of the game is that they give you different pieces.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:13.540
You got straight pieces, corners, little like squiggly lines.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.960
I guess they're like S's or whatever.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.560
Yeah, there really isn't a...

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.940
I feel like Tetris had a cast.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:26.200
Yeah. And this game doesn't really have a cast.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:29.300
It's just like whatever they want you to look at.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:37.560
I feel like it's a less recognizable cast, but there definitely are like repeats that I get a lot.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.100
Like the one that looks like an unfolded staple.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:42.440
Sure, sure, but there's no Joey.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:43.480
I get that all the time at the worst times.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:45.460
There's no what? Joey?

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:47.280
There's no Joey.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:49.020
Joey from Tetris?

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:52.330
Yeah, Joey, the long red line.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.220
That's what I was saying, yeah.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:58.140
Yeah. The red line is not the long one.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:02:59.780
That's blue. Oh, I don't know.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:02.720
But you knew about Joey.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:06.940
Joey takes many forms.

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:13.740
So you're given these pieces and you got to kind of just place them at what might seem like randomly, right?

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:21.140
Like it's not like pipe dream where you had to connect from one source to make a closed loop.

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:26.980
This is like just place them where you want and then make sure that you close them up on the way out.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:32.480
So you can have a bunch of like half thought out ideas that like will eventually connect.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.880
You can kind of like start something and be like, nah, you know what?

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:36.940
This one's getting out of control.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:40.440
I'm not going to finish it. I'm going to go work on something else and hope I can finish this later.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:46.280
But once a loop is closed, it disappears, gives you points and frees up that space.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:53.160
So now you have more space to work on your other things because you're not getting the pieces in the order you necessarily want.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:58.380
And again, this is all like standard stuff for these puzzle games.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:08.440
But I ask you guys straight off the bat is loops different enough from these other puzzle games to keep you intrigued.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:14.640
Uh, it's different enough to at least to get me intrigued, keeping me intrigued.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:16.040
Uh, I don't know.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:27.540
Jury's out. We'll say because I, I guess, you know, I guess this is maybe like a little bit of a spoiler, but like, I guess, no, like I wasn't intrigued from, for super long.

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:37.860
Um, I think part of it is that it, you sort of are playing against yourself a lot here where there's not like, as long as you're putting pieces down, like the time never runs out.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:39.880
Like you, the only way to lose is to lose.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:48.560
And you know, I, I'm never, I've never, I don't think I've ever on this podcast advocated for a timer or like a time limit, but I do feel like this game should end.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:52.820
Now, when I'm playing on like the, the easiest level, I could go forever.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:54.980
And then when I'm playing on the hardest level, yes, it is.

00:04:54.980 --> 00:05:04.160
It does go very quickly, but I think something about it just being like indefinite makes me kind of like lose interest after a while.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:05.360
It's like, all right, I'm just doing this.

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:07.300
I don't know if that makes any sense.

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:09.740
Uh, yeah. Yeah. I, I, okay.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:13.840
So first of all, I would say that this game does have a timer.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:18.940
Like it's not the, but it is just a timer in which to place something.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:27.640
Right. And after a long enough timeframe, like placing something does mean kind of breaking whatever you're working towards.

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:30.600
So I, I, I will grant it that.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:44.760
Um, I think what this game, what, what this puzzle and not, not so much the game, uh, is, is lacking is some amount of information.

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:58.940
Like there's definitely a lot of randomness that gets jumped in here and it, it probably would work out better if there was a little bit more player information.

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:01.480
I feel like it needs to tell you what piece is coming next.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:05.920
That is exactly what I'm, I'm, I was talking about and yeah, you're right.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:07.220
Like that, that is what it needed.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:09.360
Like there needed to be some kind of Tetris.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:10.540
Like this is the next piece.

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:13.240
And then you can work off that.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:21.380
Otherwise you're just mostly trying not, you're only focused on not boxing yourself out.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:27.960
And that really isn't conducive to feeling like you have laid a grand, a grand plan.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:34.940
Yeah. More often than not, my plans are like, I have, I have one thing in one corner, another thing in another corner.

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:40.300
And I'm always just like, all right, as soon as I get this piece, whenever that may be, I'll put it here.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:46.540
But yeah, after over time, it's like, well, now I'm running out of places to put all these other pieces I'm getting that aren't the piece I need.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.660
So it would be, yeah, it would be nice to be able to plan like one step ahead.

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:53.520
And then that makes it more like, yeah, it gives you more agency in that way.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:07:02.180
And I do think that the game achieves this idea of, you know, oh, this is, if you play on the very first level, like Joe was saying, like, oh, this is a very easy game.

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:05.240
I'm, I'm, I'm in complete control.

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:10.740
And I just, as long as I don't make a, play something where it shouldn't be, I'll always make loops.

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:20.700
And then it like, as it gets faster or as you have less time to choose where the loops go, or you get too many pieces that are, aren't completed loops on the board.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:24.120
Suddenly it's like, well, shit, I did screw up after all.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:27.280
Like, it's kind of like, um, like any good puzzle game.

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:33.280
It's something that, uh, if you, if you're not staying on top of it, you're not constantly closing loops.

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:35.460
Uh, you are going to be in trouble.

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:43.120
Yeah. When you get to the point where you're just finding a place to put something and it has nothing to do with what your actual goal is.

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:50.120
I think like you're kind of cooked, but it, I mean that it kind of works out.

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:58.060
It kind of feels like the game has at least set a standard for what you should be working towards.

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:00.840
And you know that you have not met that standard.

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:07.100
And, uh, you now know, you now have something to, to, to kind of work towards.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:12.220
And maybe that doesn't make a lot of sense, but it, it does in my brain at the moment.

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:13.200
Yeah. Well, yeah.

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:25.660
And sometimes I do have like, I find satisfaction that I wasn't looking for when I end up having to make a bunch of random junk on, you know, off of my plan.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:27.220
And then I loop that.

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:28.300
And I'm like, okay, well not.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:34.380
So like it, there is, I do like the satisfaction of like taking this negative thing and turning it into like a victory.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:41.120
Um, when you can, when you can pull that off, it's, it adds a little something to it.

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:51.120
And then there's also plenty of times where I'm pretty much white, like every time I play, I'm like, I'm going to make the biggest, most convoluted loop.

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:53.220
I have it in my head exactly what I'm going to make.

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:59.180
And then after like, after like a few, you know, placements, I'm like, okay, I'll make two big loops.

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:03.640
And then after a while I'm like, okay, I'm making five tiny loops that'll give me like a few points each.

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:06.920
So like every single time that's kind of like process.

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:12.060
So it does kind of throw you for a loop, uh, depending on what you're planning.

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:17.740
And the whole game presentationally is, is, is going for functional rather than flashy.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:30.200
Uh, you know, there's the monochromatic loop pieces, which are, you know, could use like, I feel like it wouldn't hurt if it had the Tetris thing of like, you know, some pieces are blue, some pieces are red, some pieces are purple.

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:35.380
And it just like helps differentiate and make the, you know, add some color to the game.

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:48.920
But even the, you know, the grid background is like, it's all solid green with like, you know, it's got something, it's got some kind of texture thing going on, but it's like designed to keep you like focused in on your loops or whatever.

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:52.440
The UI is basically non-existent.

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:57.060
And I, you know, there's a score on one side, a timer in the middle, a timer bar rather.

00:09:57.060 --> 00:09:59.300
And, um, what's that?

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:02.020
Like how many pieces you've laid down on the left side?

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:05.000
Yeah. It's how many loops you've created, how many loops you've created.

00:10:05.140 --> 00:10:24.000
Yeah. So it's like, you know, it could have, I feel like they could have jazzed this game up a little more because that's one thing that like pipe dream had over this is like pipe dream had like the concept of like, uh, you know, the, um, the running water flues, uh, going from one end to the other.

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:25.960
Right. It had like a motif behind it.

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:30.620
Tetris doesn't have a motif, but it also has like color and fun music.

00:10:30.620 --> 00:10:34.740
This game is missing like something fun about it.

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:44.040
And, and is more of like, uh, I don't know, like something I feel like I would experience on like a, like a PC as a built in game.

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:51.140
I almost feel like, cause we often like compare things to like a PC game or old PC game, or even like a, like a mobile game.

00:10:51.140 --> 00:10:59.140
To me, this feels even like maybe a step, a step away from that, where this feels like a mobile game on like a Motorola flip phone.

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:02.340
Doesn't it sound like it kind of feels like a mobile game?

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:03.220
No, I get it.

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:15.940
I get it. But I think it might, it might illuminate the same answers.

00:11:16.440 --> 00:11:26.600
Like, do you think that this game would be more fun if it had like Tetris effect level, like particle shit going on?

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:35.720
One, every time you let you lay down a weird piece, like is, is, are we just looking for feedback that, yeah, that kind of weird feedback?

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:44.720
Or are you looking for something that, uh, will, will give you, uh, the maximum puzzle happiness?

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:48.440
I think, I mean, I, I think a little bit of both.

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:56.980
I think before Mike said his piece, I was thinking more of, um, yeah, like a little more, a little maximum puzzle happiness as you put it.

00:11:57.100 --> 00:12:03.160
But, but I, I think I do agree with Mike too, that like, yeah, they could be, they could be spiced up visually.

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:04.980
I mean, there's not much going on in this game.

00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:09.840
So I feel like, you know, maybe you can put a little more resources into making it look nice, you know?

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:19.420
Um, because I mean, just visually it's like, yeah, it's like, it's like camouflage green and like a brownish beige.

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:25.700
Like, that's just like not like a bright fun thing for a game where you're just placing lines.

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:28.880
It's like already kind of like boring on paper.

00:12:29.060 --> 00:12:34.160
Eh, spice it up a little bit, you know, give me some color, give me some, some sound effects and stuff.

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:46.300
And if the, um, game A is the standard mode where you're just, uh, dropped in and forming loops and whatever game B and game C try to like keep the same mechanics.

00:12:46.760 --> 00:12:55.940
They don't change anything about it, but now they've introduced, uh, you know, uh, game C has the memory mode where they show you a complete loop, take it away.

00:12:55.940 --> 00:13:00.740
And then you have to reconstruct it exactly the same pieces.

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:03.140
That's not really a game.

00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:04.480
That's not fun at all.

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:06.440
It is so obvious.

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:17.400
Yeah. It's literally, you, you know how the, how this game is being solved and you just remember where the pieces went because they're only giving you enough turns as,

00:13:17.620 --> 00:13:20.760
because they show you every moment that they remove a piece.

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:36.140
And I'm not saying that it's like, that it's super, like it's super simple or it's super obvious, but like you, you can use your big boy pants to figure out like, oh, they took, they took this kind of piece away.

00:13:36.500 --> 00:13:40.940
And there's a, there's enough, uh, uh, like pattern.

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:48.020
Uh, uh, what do you call it when it's, it's the same on the same side.

00:13:48.220 --> 00:13:49.580
It's the same on each side.

00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:51.720
Symmetrical. Symmetry.

00:13:51.980 --> 00:13:53.580
Symmetry is the word I'm looking at.

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:58.240
There's enough symmetry that it's like kind of hard to fuck this up.

00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:02.980
Yeah. So I just don't think that it's that great of a, of a mode.

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:05.880
The primary mode is much better than that.

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:12.880
What's lacking with this mode for me, because I think this mode has more, maybe not more potential, but it has potential.

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:31.440
I think what's lacking to me is that is, I guess the fact that it is such a memory game kind of trivializes the puzzle element of it where I wish that they did something similar to this, but like, could they do this where you start where you don't see the full thing?

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:34.740
And maybe there's an, in some, some indication, maybe the same color somewhere.

00:14:34.840 --> 00:14:38.260
So it's like, this end has to connect to this thing over here somehow while this has to connect.

00:14:38.380 --> 00:14:40.220
So it doesn't show you what the shape looks like.

00:14:40.220 --> 00:14:44.260
You have to, you have to figure out like, how can I get this over there?

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:51.340
You know? Yeah. And then that, then, then it feels like I'm solving a puzzle rather than trying to just remember exactly what piece was where.

00:14:51.740 --> 00:15:00.660
And then game B is like similar to game A where you are given pieces and you have to make loops, but the loops don't go away.

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:03.820
Yeah. I wasn't entirely sure what the difference was here.

00:15:03.820 --> 00:15:07.740
I think it's like, you have to, you have to create as many loops as possible.

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:18.020
And then once you can't make any more, then it, um, then it like calculates your score because I was making complete loops and I was like, is it looking for a specific shape?

00:15:18.140 --> 00:15:19.640
Cause they weren't going away.

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:26.760
But then only once you, once you like think that it's game over, that's when it starts scoring off all your loops.

00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:28.440
Huh? Yeah.

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:30.180
That's interesting. Yeah.

00:15:30.340 --> 00:15:34.980
When I, when I was playing this one, I was like, I have no idea what the difference is.

00:15:35.380 --> 00:15:41.940
Well, with what we were doing before and it just seemed to be scoring differently and, uh, just, just let it do its thing.

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:44.960
So I'm going to just trust your judgment on this one.

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:02.760
Bye.

00:16:35.800 --> 00:16:40.520
You know, there's one thing we haven't talked about when it comes to laying down the pieces.

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:42.760
And that is that laying down the pieces.

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:48.360
These are blocks. That is the ability to actually delete some, too.

00:16:48.560 --> 00:16:52.600
Granted, you need a specific, you need the gear icon one for that.

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:59.080
But you can delete an uncompleted loop that maybe you made like really long or something on purpose.

00:16:59.080 --> 00:17:02.100
So this way you can delete all of it because you can't choose what you want to delete.

00:17:02.200 --> 00:17:03.960
You don't draw over the area you want to delete.

00:17:04.100 --> 00:17:07.620
You just click on the line and it says, OK, that's all gone.

00:17:07.900 --> 00:17:09.840
It's nice. It's a good feature.

00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:16.200
Again, it's just one of those things, though, too, where it's like that's another piece that you have to kind of like bank on getting.

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:18.100
And there's no information on if it's coming.

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:22.560
I feel like you should just get like one or two of those per game.

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:25.560
And you just press, you know, like the select button or something.

00:17:25.560 --> 00:17:27.260
And that brings it up instead.

00:17:27.700 --> 00:17:28.840
So now you have that.

00:17:28.940 --> 00:17:31.320
But it's like, be careful because now you're out of those, you know.

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:36.800
And just just to clarify, because I don't think that I'm the smartest like puzzle person.

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:42.560
I will say that there were moments where I thought I was just dumping a piece.

00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:45.680
And just like, oh, I find a place for this piece fits.

00:17:45.940 --> 00:17:47.720
And then it would just complete a loop.

00:17:48.180 --> 00:17:51.140
And I did not know how I succeeded there.

00:17:51.140 --> 00:17:55.080
And I think that that's like that's like a really bad thing.

00:17:55.480 --> 00:17:58.120
If that happens. Like, did that happen to you guys at all?

00:17:58.180 --> 00:18:00.160
It's just like, oh, I thought I was just putting this where it fit.

00:18:00.660 --> 00:18:02.520
I feel like the reverse happened to me.

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:05.140
I thought I was completing a loop and it did.

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:06.100
It did not happen.

00:18:06.300 --> 00:18:10.820
OK. So I don't really know what this is a reflection of.

00:18:11.080 --> 00:18:15.980
If it's just us being dumb or us not understanding the rules.

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:18.460
It's almost definitely us not understanding the rules.

00:18:18.880 --> 00:18:20.460
But let's just pretend it isn't.

00:18:20.740 --> 00:18:26.780
Go on. If I'm understanding what you're saying, you put it somewhere not realizing that it does complete a loop.

00:18:27.760 --> 00:18:32.240
Yeah, because I have a very specific like concept of what's a loop.

00:18:32.700 --> 00:18:36.960
And it would just be like, oh, this is kind of like a line that might intersect itself.

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:38.480
Oh, OK. So it's not.

00:18:38.580 --> 00:18:39.800
